{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>A man, his mother.</b> It mentions the mother first, because he recognizes her first. And afterwards — Shabbat — for there is a commandment upon the father to keep the minor [in the observance of] Shabbat. And afterwards — he recognizes HaShem (הקב״ה), who rested on the seventh day. And after Him: 'do not turn to the idols' — so as not to deny the fundamental principle." "<b>And My Sabbaths you shall keep.</b> Do as I did — I rested on it from all labor. And just as in the Ten Commandments it says 'honor father and mother' next to the keeping of Shabbat — thus equating their honor with the honor of HaShem (הקב״ה) — so too here. And therefore they are juxtaposed, so that you not say: since Scripture equated the honor of father and mother with the honor of HaShem (המקום) — as it is written כבד את אביך etc., and it is written כבד את ה׳ מהונך — if his father and mother tell him to violate Shabbat, that he is obligated to do [i.e., he need not obey them]; even though honoring a father is only a positive commandment, and keeping Shabbat is a positive and a negative commandment, since Scripture equated their honor to the honor of HaShem (המקום), it is established [that he need not obey them]. All this is explained in the chapter 'Eilu Metzi'ot.' And there is a difficulty: why does the verse need it? For the juxtaposition is not to create stringency but to make him like Him, and if so, from where do we know that one should push aside the honor of HaShem (המקום) for the sake of the honor of his father and mother? One may answer: because the demand of his father and mother is greater than that of HaShem (המקום) — for regarding HaShem (המקום) it says כבד את ה׳ מהונך, meaning 'if he has [means]'; but regarding his father and mother, even if he does not have, he is obligated to go begging from door to door for their honor. Thus explained the Tosafot. And yet it remains difficult, for it is written ונשיא בעמך לא תאור, and if his father and mother tell him to desecrate Shabbat he is not obligated to honor them. One may answer: it speaks of a rabbinic shvut, for the honor of father and mother, which is Torah-law, pushes aside a rabbinic shvut."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "In the phrase 'that he is obligated to do,' the referent is ambiguous; read as 'he is obligated to keep Shabbat [and need not obey them].' The phrase קמ״ל resolved as 'קא מפקע ליה' per standard idiom in this sugya."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:13:27.283431730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}